.fill.justify.offset 5.rightmargin 55.pagesize 48.m1 2.m2 1.m3 2.m4 3.ec \.header /Utmp/OS9Lib/Utmp.footer /Page #//Printed (%)/.in +5.define topic.bold.in -5$1 $2 $3 $4 $5.in +5.en.define endtopic.en.define noform.nojustify.nofill.en.define noformend.justify.fill.en.! Manual created with MWB.! END-OF-MACRO.topic NAMEUtmp - Library to examine the /dd/SYS/utmp file.endtopic.topic SYNOPSIS.noformint	_utmp_stat (stat)char	*stat;int _utmp_write (type, name, line, pid, status, uid, gid)int	type;char	*name;char	*line;int	pid;char	*status;int	uid;int	gid;struct utmp	*getutent ()int	setutent ()int	endutent ()struct utmp	*getutid (id)struct utmp	*id;struct utmp	*getutline (line)struct utmp	*line;int	pututline (utmp)struct utmp	*utmp;void	utmpname (file)char	*file;.noformend.endtopic.topic DESCRIPTIONThe utmp-library offers a set of functionsfor easy access to the.ul/dd/SYS/utmpfile or files which have the same structure (e.g. /h0/SYS/wtmp). For thesefunction there is a.ulstruct utmpdefinied in utmp.h:.nf.nj.in +5struct utmp {  /* User login name                */  char   ut_user[33];  /* /etc/lines id (usually line #) */  char   ut_id[4];  /* device name (console, lnxx)    */  char   ut_line[33];  /* process id                     */  short  ut_pid;  /* User ID, Group ID              */  short  ut_uid, ut_gid;  /* Baud rate                      */  int    ut_baud;  /* type of entry                  */  short  ut_type;  /* time entry was made            */  time_t ut_time;  /* Flags                          */  char   ut_status[10];};.in -5.justify.fill	To initialize the access to /dd/SYS/utmp you should call thefunction.boldsetutent()which opens or rewinds the file. When you have finished your access tothis file you should call.boldendutent()to close the file. The function.boldgeturent()fetches the next entry of the utmp file and returns a pointer to thestruct utmp which has been filled by this function..boldGetutid()requires a struct utmp where the entry.ulut_typeis set to the value you look for,.boldgetutline()needs also a struct utmp with.ulut_lineset to the required contents. With.boldpututline()an entry will be added in a free slot of the current file..boldUtmpname()requires a file name and all other operations then take place on thisfile instead of the /dd/SYS/utmp.	There also exist some internal function which may be useful forsome reasons..bold_utmp_stat()requires a string with flags and changes the current flags for thisline..bold_utmp_write()is the most used internal function. The struct utmp is filled up withthe arguments and then the entry will be written to the utmp file orthey will be deleted from there if.ultypeis EMPTY (definied in utmp.h)..endtopic.topic CAVEATSThe functions getutent(), getutid() and getutline() return a pointer tostatic data which will be overwritten by each call of one of thesefunctions..endtopic.topic RETURN VALUE_utmp_stat(), _utmp_write(), setutent(), endutent(), pututline()returning 0 on success, -1 on failure.  Getutent(), getutid(),getutline() return a pointer to struct utmp on success, NULL onfailure. .endtopic.topic FILES.noform/dd/SYS/utmp.in +5The standard utmp file..in -5.noformend.endtopic.topic SEE ALSO.noformLogon and mmonin the Documentation of the Top Release..noformend.endtopic